From: TB710FU clean board
Subject: [PATCH] Bracket the TTBR1 switch with identity-mapped markers

Diagnostic only; a boot without the argument redirects nothing.

The reporter records stage 47 on entry to set_mmu_ttb_alternate and never draws
again. The next traced call site exists and every instruction between the return
address and it is mapped and executable, so control never leaves the switch.

Those four instructions are fetched through TTBR1, and the ISB makes the table
written by the MSR effective for the very next fetch. If the new table does not
describe the kernel text, the fault lands there, and the reporter - running under
that same table - cannot draw again. That is exactly the observed frozen screen
with no exception report.

A marker written by ordinary compiled code cannot be trusted here: a C version
using two volatile byte stores compiled into an offset store plus a reordered
constant, and any code placed after the MSR is fetched through the table under
test. The stub below is a single literal asm block that emits the exact sequence
with the address built by movz/movk, so the compiler has no freedom over an
experiment whose entire value is the exact address and ordering of two stores.

The markers are stored through the low-half 1:1 map at physical 0x93ffff000,
the top page of the 2 GiB bootstrap window - above topOfKernelData and above the
new page tables, so no kernel allocation can occupy it. set_mmu_ttb_alternate
changes TTBR1 only, so the bootstrap TTBR0 map translates these stores
identically on both sides of the MSR:

    mark0 = 1   stored before the TTBR1 write
    mark1 = 2   stored after the ISB

    mark0 set, mark1 clear -> execution stopped in the MSR or the ISB, which
                              points at the new table contents
    both set               -> the switch completed and the fault is later
    neither set            -> the stub was never entered

Read the bytes back from Android or TWRP: dd if=/dev/mem bs=1 \
    skip=$((0x93ffff000)) count=2
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
@@ -84,2 +84,33 @@
  */
+
+/*
+ * TB710FU diagnostics: the TTBR1 switch with identity-mapped markers.
+ *
+ * One literal asm block on purpose. A C version with two volatile byte stores
+ * was compiled into `sturb w9, [x8, #-1]` with the constant for the second
+ * store hoisted ahead of the barriers, i.e. the markers did not bracket the MSR
+ * in the emitted code. The sequence below is fixed at assembly time so the two
+ * stores are guaranteed to sit on either side of the TTBR1 write, at the exact
+ * physical address, through the low-half identity map that the switch does not
+ * touch.
+ */
+static void
+set_mmu_ttb_alternate_probed(uint64_t ttbr1)
+{
+	__asm__ volatile(
+	    "movz x8, #0xf000\n"
+	    "movk x8, #0x3fff, lsl #16\n"
+	    "movk x8, #0x9, lsl #32\n"
+	    "movz w9, #1\n"
+	    "strb w9, [x8]\n"
+	    "dsb sy\n"
+	    "msr TTBR1_EL1, %[ttbr1]\n"
+	    "isb\n"
+	    "movz w9, #2\n"
+	    "strb w9, [x8, #1]\n"
+	    :
+	    : [ttbr1] "r"(ttbr1)
+	    : "x8", "w9", "memory");
+}
+
 SECURITY_READ_ONLY_LATE(uint8_t) bootstrap_pagetables[BOOTSTRAP_TABLE_SIZE] __attribute__((aligned(ARM_PGBYTES)));
@@ -2544,3 +2544,12 @@
 	arm_vm_physmap_init(args);
-	set_mmu_ttb_alternate(cpu_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
+	/* TB710FU diagnostics: marked switch when the boot argument asks for it. */
+	{
+		uint32_t switch_marks = 0;
+		if (PE_parse_boot_argn("TB710FU_SWITCH_MARKS", &switch_marks,
+		    sizeof(switch_marks)) && switch_marks != 0) {
+			set_mmu_ttb_alternate_probed(cpu_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
+		} else {
+			set_mmu_ttb_alternate(cpu_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
+		}
+	}
 
